# From SŪTRADHĀRA — the NULL-fill gate is built and correct, and it blocks on 59 cells, not 1

M0-T35 landed (`326e4cb37`). Both halves of D-27/D-28 part 1 are implemented, and the reasoning
on the one judgment call is better than the instruction I gave it.

**Marked per my new practice:** the counts below are **the KĀRAKA's measurement, relayed, not
independently re-derived by me**. What I *did* verify myself is the live NULL distribution across
the columns the seed writes on conflict — 621 NULL cells across 11 columns, led by `provides_apis`
126, `health_probe` 126, `estimated_seconds` 119, `expected_volume_formula` 108, `size_sql` 51,
`volume_explanation` 44. **I have NOT verified the per-column breakdown of the 59 transitions**;
that requires modelling what the seed would write per cell, which is precisely what its
`deriveSeedRow()` does. The query that would settle it is a join of that projection against the
live NULLs, grouped by column.

## `target_floor` was REMOVED, not guarded — and the argument is why

I offered removal or an MR-06-style `CASE` guard and told it to argue its choice. It read the
MR-06 guard first and chose against copying it:

> MR-06 works because `RETIRED` is a **terminal state readable in the row**. *"Is this floor
> MEASURED?"* has no answer in the row at all — `asset_registry` stores no provenance for
> `target_floor`, so there is nothing a `CASE` could read.

And the obvious stand-in makes it worse: `IS NOT NULL` asks *"is it set?"*, which would still
write a typed number into the three live-NULL floors — **the exact stale-figure write I7 forbids,
and itself a NULL→value fill.** Guard leaves 3 bad cells; removal leaves 0.

It also cited your D-28 part 4: **MR-06 has never had to choose**, so a second guard modelled on
it would copy a shape nothing has ever separated. That is your own finding used as an argument
against reusing the pattern, which is the ledger working as case law rather than as decoration.

`target_floor` stays in the **INSERT** list — a never-built asset has no measurement to collide
with — and it flagged that as a residual rather than accepting it silently.

## The gate blocks on 59 cells and it is your ruling that makes it so

Measured: **59 NULL→value · 2 value→NULL · 133 value→value · 53 declared-but-verifiable.** All 8
`asset_kind`/`asset_type` reverting rows named, every one with `key_declared=false`. Both
`lel_events` cells categorised `null_to_value` and annotated *"[from a `??` default]"*.

The gate **blocks** and takes a **per-cell** `SEED_ACK_NULL_FILL` list. Its reasoning for blocking
rather than warning: a NULL fill is unrecoverable, because the row becomes indistinguishable from
a correct one — so a warning is a mechanism with no consequence. And its reasoning against a
blanket flag: that is the scrolled-past warning in a costume. A stale token matching nothing in
the current report also blocks.

**So the first real seed run is blocked behind a 59-token acknowledgement list.** The KĀRAKA
raised this and **did not narrow it**, on the grounds that choosing which NULLs are "deliberate" is
the judgment D-28 reserved and **your word was "any"**. It observes that most of the 59 are
probably ordinary absent data rather than reserved decisions, and suggests a column-level
allowance may be worth a ruling.

I think it was right not to decide, and I think the question is real: the gate as built is
faithful to D-28 and operationally heavy, and those are not in tension — they are just two
different things to weigh, and the weighing is yours. If a column-level allowance is right, the
distinguishing property is probably *whether a NULL in that column can ever encode a reserved
decision*, which for `volume_explanation` it plausibly cannot and for `layer_index` it demonstrably
can.

## Two properties worth keeping

**The report's column coverage is parsed from the upsert statement itself**, so it cannot drift
from the `SET` clause — the class of defect that produced three stale control surfaces today,
designed out rather than watched for. And `deriveSeedRow()` is now the single place the `??`
defaults live, so the report and the bound parameters read the same objects; the `?? 'data'` trap
that made the reversion count 8 rather than 6 cannot now diverge between what is reported and what
is written.

**No `lel_events` special case**, per D-25 and D-28 part 1: the gate fires on the shape, and a test
strips comments and asserts no asset id appears in any code path.

